  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH TONIGHT...Early morning satellite and radar \nimagery together with surface obs showed a textbook Mesoscale \nconvective system (MCS) with a nearly symmetric cloud shield \nand expansive footprint of precipitation. As of 11am, the back \nof this precipitation was clearing I-35, and this trend will \ncontinue to the east the rest of the morning into the early \nafternoon.\n\nLooking ahead, the chance for rain to redevelop later today and\nthis evening is low, so do have precipitation chances dropping\nfrom near 100 percent this morning, to roughly 40 percent later\ntoday. \n\nThe risk for severe weather is low with storms, as the weak\nsurface low will allow the cold pool to remain across the\nregion, and the lack of strong synoptic forcing means the\ninstability to the south won't be advected northward. Similarly,\nthe threat for flooding rain will be south of the region as\nwell, closer to the higher instability, where rainfall rates \nwill be greatest due to convection.\n\nIn summary, there is a non-zero chance for flooding and severe \nweather, but SPC maintains the Marginal risk to the south, and\nthe HiRes Guidance trends shown via the 13.12Z HREF have the\nheaviest rainfall near I-80 in central Iowa.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH SATURDAY...The boundary responsible for this \nrepeated rounds of thunderstorms will move very little. It may \noscillate slightly northward during the day, but then get pushed\nsouthward as the cold pool develops from overnight convection. \n\nThis remains the case for Friday into Saturday. There are\nchances for thunderstorms in the forecast, but the higher\nchances are to the south. The only difference is the upper level\nridge shifts eastward a bit, meaning there is some southwest\ncomponent to the upper level jet. This could push the storms a\nbit farther north as compared to tonight's placement.\n\nAgain, the risk for severe weather is low, with heavy rain and\nlocalized flooding as the primary threat.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY...No major changes from the previous\nforecast. Weak high pressure builds across the region as the\nupper level jet becomes northwest oriented once again. The\nhighest probability for thunderstorms remain across central\nIowa, but predictability is lower in this set up as light winds\nremain in place the the surface features are subtle at best.\nTemperature remain seasonal, with highs near 80, and lows near\n60.",
